bb <- st_bbox(my.sf)

grat <- st_graticule(
  x = bb,
  crs = st_crs(my.sf),
  lon = seq(floor(bb["xmin"]/50)*50, ceiling(bb["xmax"]/50)*50, by = 50),
  lat = seq(floor(bb["ymin"]/20)*20, ceiling(bb["ymax"]/20)*20, by = 20)
)

num.areas <- nrow(my.sf)

@

\title{A dataset of forest cover and change 2000–2020 aggregated to administrative areas for social and environmental research}
\author{Hans Ekbrand}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\abstract{We present a dataset of forest cover and forest cover change for 2000–2020 in low- and middle-income countries, constructed by aggregating high-resolution satellite-derived land cover data to \num{\Sexpr{num.areas}} subnational administrative areas. These spatial units are socially and politically meaningful and designed to be compatible with geocoded household survey data from the Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS), while also supporting integration with other datasets.

The dataset includes baseline forest cover in 2000 and changes in forest cover at five-year intervals up to 2020. By eliminating the need for spatial data processing, it enables researchers to analyse deforestation and forest gain using standard statistical tools.}

\section{Motivation}
Over the last 10.000 years, humans have reshaped large portions of the Earth's surface by cutting down trees and replaced them with crops, substituting forests with farmlands. The last few decades satellite images covering the land surface of the entire globe have been regularly produced. Researchers have developed models that can infer from these satellite images the extent of forest and other types of land and in 2013 a database of forest coverage and deforestation for the entire Earth during the period 2000-2013 was published by \citet{Hansen2013}, and it has been updated regularly \citep{Hansen_GlobalForestChange_2024}. The data points in this database represent very small rectangles, approximately $30\times30 \textrm{m}^2$. Figure \vref{fig:Hansen} is a visualisation of that database. In short, the motivation of this paper and the accompanying data products is to provide forest change data in an aggregated format that is easily usable for social scientists.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{hansen.png}
    \caption{Visualisation of the Global Forest Change database 2000-2024. Source: Hansen/UMD/Google/USGS/NASA. Green represent stable forests, red is areas with deforestation, blue is new forest and pink is a mix of deforestation followed by new forest growth.}
    \label{fig:Hansen}
\end{figure}

\section{Spatial aggregation}
\label{sec:spatial-aggregation}

If you want to explain \emph{why} deforestation takes place, or describe and explain the \emph{social consequences} of deforestation, you need to put the deforestation data in context, and for this purpose you need data aggregated to some socially relevant area unit. A natural choice for such areas are the administrative areas defined by each country. Aggregating time series data from 212 billion 30×30 m grid cells into \num{\Sexpr{num.areas}} administrative areas is essential for analysing the causes and consequences of deforestation. The computational and technical challenges involved motives us to make the results of the aggregation publicly available in an accessible format, hence this paper.

As our primary interest lies in the connections between \emph{deforestation and poverty}, we have focused our computational resources to do the analyses for low- and middle income countries (mainly in Latin America, Africa, and South Asia). In fact, we have used geographical availability of micro data on living conditions from the Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) to guide the choice of spatial units, making the data product particularly suitable for users of DHS data \citet{Wang2021}. However, the algorithms we have developed are flexible in two ways: (1) they work with any partitioning, (2) they work with any part of the world (not only the southern hemisphere). Given an alternative partitioning of some area of the Earth, quite a bit of disk space, internet access and some computation time, alternative versions of the data products can easily be produced. This document both serves as a manual for how to use the aggregated database, and as the canonical source for how it was done.\footnote{The PDF is build by the same program that produces the data products, and the source code for this program is publicly available here \citep{deforestation_repo}} Figure \ref{fig:full-grid} visualizes the partitioning we developed and based the data product on.

<<full-grid, fig.cap = "Administrative divisions used in the analysis.", dpi = 400, fig.width=6, fig.height=2.4>>=
library(sf); library(ggplot2)
## par(mar = c(0, 0, 0, 0))
## plot(st_geometry(full.grid.sf), lwd = 0.05)

ggplot() +
    ## graticule behind polygons
    geom_sf(data = grat, linewidth = 0.10) +
    ## The adminiatrative borders
    geom_sf(data = my.sf, fill = "white", linewidth = 0.05) + 

    ## lock extent to the bbox
    coord_sf(
        xlim = c(bb["xmin"], bb["xmax"]),
        ylim = c(bb["ymin"], bb["ymax"]),
        expand = FALSE
    ) +

  theme_minimal()

@

\section{Data Source}
\label{sec:source}

The data we aggregate is \citet{Potapov2022}, which provide high resolution data about land cover and land use change for every 5 year from 2000 to 2020. While \citet{Hansen_GlobalForestChange_2024} provide annual data, with more recent updates, the updates only include forest loss, not forest gain. Forest \emph{gain} data in \citet{Hansen_GlobalForestChange_2024} only cover 2001 - 2012, which makes \citet{Potapov2022} the better choice if both forest loss and forest gain is of interest.

\section{Definitions}
\label{sec:definitions}

In this database, and in this document at large, \emph{forest coverage} has a precise definition: the percentage of the area that is covered by organic structures (trees) that reflects sunlight at least 5 meters above the ground. We thereby follow the definition developed by \citet{Hansen2013}, but we apply it on another data source.

\emph{change in forestation} is defined as change in \emph{forest coverage} and we express it in percentage points. If 70 \% of an area was covered forest at time t0, and at time t1 65 \% of the area is covered by forest, the change in forestation of that area between time t0 and t1 was -5. The recorded \emph{change in forestation} statistic is this \emph{difference in forestation}. This difference is the value at t1 minus the value at t0, so a negative value indicates deforestation and a positive value indicate forest gain. Gain is thus here defined only with reference to the extent of area covered by forest, not to the height of the trees in the forest. We explicitly mention this because the data source we use, \citet{Potapov2022}, do provide estimated average canopy height, we simply do not use the vertical aspect of forest gain, only the horizontal aspect.

The spatial aggregation does not add any complexity to the definitions involved, the forest coverage of an area is simply the \emph{average value} of all the cells in the original source that fall inside the area.

\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

This section will provide a conceptual overview of the data processing pipeline, without going into technical details of the implementation, see the source code for those \citep{deforestation_repo}.

\subsection{Principles  guiding the partitioning}
\label{sec:creat-part}
Almost all countries provide a tree-like hierarchy of administrative borders, there are top level regions, which in turn have sub-divisions, which in turn can have their own subdivisions, and these are all made publicly available by the GADM project \citet{Hijmans_GADM_v41}. Most countries use a hierarchy with two or three levels, and as a general principle, we start with the finest layer of sub-divisions available for each country, but not deeper than level 3. If the country has DHS surveys, then the partitioning is guided by the availability of DHS clusters with GPS coordinates in each area. The aim is to have a partitioning suitable for time series analyses and reasonable minimal set of data points (collected with significant distance in time) is thus 3. Areas with less than 3 time-variant data points are, if possible, successively merged with another incomplete area with which it shares both parent and one border, until a partitioning where all areas contain at least 3 DHS clusters, or no further mergers are possible. The partitioning described above is a built-in feature of the «R« package «globallivingconditions« \citet{Ekbrand_dhsharmonisation}. For this project, we combined the partitions provided by that R package with the finest layers of sub-divisions for the countries with no DHS surveys. For countries with no or only partial coverage by DHS surveys the finest level for administrative areas is the most sensible choice since if aggregation to a higher level is needed to fit national statistics it is easy to achieve using information in the GADM files (the files that describe the lower level areas also include information about their parents in the tree).

\subsection{Principles for aggregation}
\label{sec:princ-aggr}

To illustrate the principles used for aggregation consider an artificial example of a tiny administrative area for which we can actually see the values recorded for each cell in the source data, see figure \vref{fig:sample-area}.\footnote{This shape in the example is from an actual administrative area, but the cells size are enlargened and the the values in the cells are randomized.}

<<sample-area, fig.cap = "Illustration of the format of source data and an administrative area.", fig.width = 3, fig.height = 3, fig.align = "center">>=
library(sf)
library(ggplot2)
library(readr)
library(dplyr)

# --- real bbox -------------------------------------------------------------

bbox <- st_bbox(c(
  xmin = 0.4260,
  ymin = 35.8211,
  xmax = 0.6497,
  ymax = 35.9913
), crs = st_crs(my.sf))

# --- 5 x 5 grid ------------------------------------------------------------

grid <- st_make_grid(
  st_as_sfc(bbox),
  n = c(6, 6)
) |>
  st_sf()

# --- read valid values and colors -----------------------------------------

lut <- read_csv("color-scheme-glclu.csv", show_col_types = FALSE) |>
  rename(value = 1, hex = 2) |>
  mutate(
    hex = trimws(hex),
    hex = ifelse(hex == "", NA, hex),
    hex = paste0("#", hex)
  ) |>
  filter(!is.na(hex))

# sample only from valid values
set.seed(1)
these <- which(sapply(lut[, 2], nchar) == 7)
grid$value <- sample(lut$value[these], size = nrow(grid), replace = TRUE)

# join the corresponding hex color to each sampled value
grid <- grid |>
  left_join(lut, by = "value")

# named vector for scale_fill_manual()
fill_vals <- setNames(lut$hex, lut$value)

## find the polygon that is inside the bbox (my.sf may have been re-ordered)
## convert bbox to polygon
bbox_poly <- st_as_sfc(bbox)
## find which polygons are fully within the bbox
## Temporary turn off s2.
old <- sf::sf_use_s2()
sf::sf_use_s2(FALSE)
inside <- st_within(my.sf, bbox_poly, sparse = FALSE)[, 1]
this <- which(inside)
sf::sf_use_s2(old)

# --- plot -----------------------------------------------------------------

ggplot() +
  geom_sf(data = grid, aes(fill = factor(value)), colour = "grey40", linewidth = 0.3) +
    geom_sf(data = my.sf[this, ], fill = NA, colour = "red", linewidth = 1.1) +

  # black text
  geom_sf_text(
    data = grid[grid$value <= 200, ],
    aes(label = value),
    colour = "black",
    size = 3
  ) +

  # white text for dark cells
  geom_sf_text(
    data = grid[grid$value > 200, ],
    aes(label = value),
    colour = "white",
    size = 3
  ) +
    
  scale_fill_manual(values = fill_vals, drop = FALSE) +
  coord_sf(expand = FALSE) +
  theme_minimal() +
    labs(fill = "Value", title = NULL,
         x = NULL, y = NULL) +
    theme(legend.position = "none",
          axis.text = element_text(size = 8))

@ 

The forest coverage is based on the number of valid cells in the area and here we do not include cells that represent ``open surface water'' (values 200-207) or ``Ocean'' (value 254). The second statistic needed is the number of forest cells, cells that represent trees with average canopy height 5m or higher (values 27-48 and 127-148). For cells that only partly intersect with the administrative area, the exact proportion of the cell that is in the area is estimated and used in the cell counts.\footnote{The cell counts are calculated by the R package «exactextractr« \citet{exactextractr}.} The sum of the total cell count can thus be a decimal number. The target statistic average forest coverage is then calculated by dividing the cell count of forest cells with the cell count of valid cells.

$$ \textrm{Average forest coverage} = \frac{\textrm{count of forest cells}}{\textrm{count of valid cells}} $$

The meaning of the values in the source data is given by the legend, see the project page for the source data: \href{https://storage.googleapis.com/earthenginepartners-hansen/GLCLU2000-2020/v2/download.html}{Global Land Cover and Land Use 2000 and 2020}. The average valid cell count for the administrative areas is \num{2429552}, and the total number of valid cells are \num{83391942848}.

%% Borders needed for the next graph.

<<forest.change.path.to.artifacts>>=
path.to.artifacts <- "/mnt/windows/Deforestation and poverty - Paper I/"
@ 

<<forest.change.import.borders>>=
all.borders <- readRDS(file.path(path.to.artifacts, "all.borders.RDS"))

my.crop.f <- function(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax, my.sf){

  withCallingHandlers({

    old <- sf::sf_use_s2()
    on.exit(sf::sf_use_s2(old), add = TRUE)
    sf::sf_use_s2(FALSE)

    bbox <- sf::st_bbox(
      c(xmin = xmin, ymin = ymin, xmax = xmax, ymax = ymax),
      crs = sf::st_crs(my.sf)
    )

    sf::st_crop(my.sf, bbox)

  }, warning = function(w) invokeRestart("muffleWarning"))
}

@


<<quantify.boundary.to.interior>>=
make_snapped_raster <- function(x, resolution = 1 / 3600, crs = "EPSG:4326") {
  # Accept either a bbox-like object or a spatial object with an extent
  if (inherits(x, "SpatVector") || inherits(x, "SpatRaster") || inherits(x, "SpatExtent")) {
    e <- ext(x)
    xmin <- xmin(e)
    xmax <- xmax(e)
    ymin <- ymin(e)
    ymax <- ymax(e)
  } else if (inherits(x, "bbox")) {
    xmin <- x["xmin"]
    xmax <- x["xmax"]
    ymin <- x["ymin"]
    ymax <- x["ymax"]
  } else if (is.numeric(x) && length(x) == 4) {
    # assume c(xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax)
    xmin <- x[1]
    xmax <- x[2]
    ymin <- x[3]
    ymax <- x[4]
  } else {
    stop("x must be a SpatVector, SpatRaster, SpatExtent, bbox, or numeric vector of length 4")
  }

  # Snap outward to the resolution grid
  xmin_snap <- floor(xmin / resolution) * resolution
  xmax_snap <- ceiling(xmax / resolution) * resolution
  ymin_snap <- floor(ymin / resolution) * resolution
  ymax_snap <- ceiling(ymax / resolution) * resolution

  rast(
    xmin = xmin_snap,
    xmax = xmax_snap,
    ymin = ymin_snap,
    ymax = ymax_snap,
    resolution = resolution,
    crs = crs
  )
}

count_boundary_cells <- function(i) {
    sf_row <- my.sf[i, ]
    v <- vect(st_geometry(sf_row))

    ## snapped raster
    r <- make_snapped_raster(v)

    ## initialize output raster
    m <- rast(r)
    values(m) <- NA

    ## 1) cells touched by polygon boundary
    l <- as.lines(v)
    edge_ids <- which(!is.na(values(rasterize(l, r, field = 1, touches = TRUE))))
    m[edge_ids] <- 1

    ## 2) cells whose centers fall inside polygon
    inside_ids <- which(!is.na(values(rasterize(v, r, field = 1))))
    m[inside_ids] <- 2

    ## approximate number of fully inside polygon,
    fully.inside <- round(length(inside_ids) - length(edge_ids)/2)
  
    return(boundary_share = length(edge_ids) / fully.inside)
}

library(future.apply)
future::plan(multicore)
ratio.boundary.to.interior <- unlist(future_lapply(sample(nrow(my.sf), 200), count_boundary_cells))

@

\section{Technical validation}
Raster cells that intersect administrative boundaries are weighted by their proportional overlap with each polygon using the exactextractr package, which implements an area-weighted extraction method. This approach avoids arbitrary assignment of boundary cells and minimizes discretization bias. The impact of boundary effects is further reduced by the relatively large size of the administrative units (average ~2.4 million cells per unit). The average ratio of number of cells on the boundary over the number of cells fully inside is approximately \Sexpr{round(mean(ratio.boundary.to.interior), 3)}, with standard deviation of \Sexpr{round(sd(ratio.boundary.to.interior), 3)}, based on a random sample of 200 areas.

The aggregation procedure does not introduce additional discretization bias from polygon assignment, because cells intersecting boundaries are weighted by their proportional overlap with each administrative unit. Any discretization error is inherited from the source raster rather than created by the aggregation step itself.

\section{Data records}
\label{sec:data-records}

The dataset described in this study is publicly available at Zenodo (10.5281/zenodo.19221532) and is provided in both spatial and tabular formats to facilitate a wide range of use cases.

The dataset is distributed in two complementary formats:

\begin{description}
\item [GeoPackage (.gpkg)] This file, «forest-change.gpkg« contains both the geometries of the administrative areas and the associated attribute data. It is suitable for use in geographic information systems (GIS) and spatial analysis workflows in software such as QGIS, ArcGIS, R, and Python.
\item [Comma-separated values (.csv)] This file, «forest\_change.csv« contains the attribute data only, without geometries. It is intended for statistical analysis and can be readily merged with external datasets using standard data processing tools.
\end{description}
Both files contain identical attribute variables and are linked through a common region identifier.

\section{Dataset description}
\label{sec:dataset-description}

\subsection{Spatial units}
\label{sec:spatial-units}

The dataset comprises \num{\Sexpr{num.areas}} administrative areas across low- and middle-income countries. Administrative boundaries are based on the GADM database (Hijmans, 2022), with modifications applied in countries where Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) data are available. In these cases, administrative units are merged where necessary to ensure sufficient spatial coverage of DHS clusters.

Each observation corresponds to a single administrative area defined by a fixed set of boundaries.
Variables

The dataset includes measures of forest cover and forest cover change, along with identifying and descriptive variables. Table \vref{tab:structure} provides a summary of all variables included in the dataset.

In brief, the dataset contains:

\begin{itemize}
\item A unique region identifier (region)
\item Country and administrative region names (country, top\_level\_region)
\item Area size in square kilometers (size\_km2)
\item Forest cover in the baseline year 2000 (forestation\_2000)
\item Changes in forest cover, expressed in percentage points relative to the year 2000, for the years 2005, 2010, 2015, and 2020 (forestation\_2005–forestation\_2020)
\end{itemize}

Forest cover is defined as the proportion of land area covered by vegetation taller than 5 meters. Forest cover change is calculated as the difference in forest cover between the specified year and the baseline year 2000.

A machine-readable data dictionary describing all variables is provided alongside the dataset.

\subsection{Region identifiers}
\label{sec:region-identifiers}

The variable region is a structured string identifier constructed by concatenating multiple components separated by periods. These components include:

\begin{itemize}
\item A country code (ISO 3166; numeric codes are used for countries with DHS coverage and alphabetic codes otherwise)
\item The name of the top-level administrative region
\item (Optional) The name of the second-level administrative region
\item The name(s) of the administrative unit(s)
 \end{itemize}

In cases where administrative areas have been merged, multiple names are included and separated by a ``+'' symbol.

\subsection{Temporal structure}
Forest cover is reported for the year 2000, and forest cover change is reported for the years 2005, 2010, 2015, and 2020. All change variables represent differences in forest cover relative to the baseline year 2000 and are expressed in percentage points.

\subsection{Data completeness}
\label{sec:data-completeness}
The dataset contains one observation for each administrative area. All variables are complete for all observations, except where noted in the accompanying data dictionary. No missing values are present in the forest cover or forest cover change variables.

\subsection{File organization}
\label{sec:file-organization}

The Zenodo archive includes the following files:
\begin{description}
\item[«forest-change\.gpkg«]  - spatial dataset (geometry and attributes)
\item[«forest\_change.csv«] - tabular dataset (attributes only)
\item[«data\_dictionary.csv«] - variable definitions and metadata
\item[«README.md«] - dataset documentation
\end{description}

\begin{table}[htbp]
    \centering
    \caption{The structure of the dataset}
    \label{tab:structure}
    \begin{tabular}{lp{9cm}}
      \toprule
      Variable name & Description \\
      \midrule
      region & A unique string identifier build by concatenating 3 or 4 parts, separated by a dot:
               (1) A country code according to ISO 3166 (numeric for the countries with DHS coverage, letters otherwise); (2) The top level region name; (3, optional) The second level region name, only included if the country provides a hierarchy of more than two levels; (last) the name of the administrative area(s) If the area is a result of mergers all area names are included, separated by a ``+'', e.g. ``116.Kâmpôt.Kampot.Koun Satv + Trapeang sangkae`` for a row representing Cambodia (numeric code 116), top level region ``Kâmpôt'' and resulting from merging the two areas ``Koun Satv'' and ``Trapeang sangkae''\\
      forestation\_2000 & the proportion of the area covered by forest in year 2000 (range:0-1) \\
      forestation\_2005 & the percentage units change in forestation between year 2000 and year 2005 (theoretical range: -100 - 100)\\
      forestation\_2010 & the percentage units change in forestation between year 2000 and year 2010 \\
      forestation\_2015 & the percentage units change in forestation between year 2000 and year 2015 \\
      forestation\_2020 & the percentage units change in forestation between year 2000 and year 2020 \\
      size\_km2 & the size of the area in square kilometers \\
      top\_level\_region & name of the top level region (same as the second field in ``region'' above) \\
      country & the country name according to the ISO 3166 \\
      \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We will provide some basic descriptive data about the whole area, visualise the reference level, that is the forestation state in year 2000, and the net change, for \num{\Sexpr{num.areas}} administrative areas. While the full potential of use cases involves linking the data set to external data sets, we can identify areas with the most deforestation, aggregate the forestation change statistic to top level regions in countries, and identify top level regions of interest. But we should also point out that, having a set of areas where deforestation took place also opens for more detailed analyses of the consequences of deforestation, see section \ref{lab:consequences}.

%\subsection{Forest coverage year 2000}
<<descriptives>>=
forestation.2000 <- my.dt[, weighted.mean(forestation.2000, w=size.km2, na.rm=TRUE), ]
deforestation.2020 <- my.dt[, weighted.mean(forestation.change.2020, w=size.km2, na.rm=TRUE), ]
forestation.2020 <- forestation.2000 + deforestation.2020
size.km2 <- my.dt[, sum(size.km2), ]
forest.area.2000 <- forestation.2000 / 100 * size.km2
forest.area.2020 <- forestation.2020 / 100 * size.km2

@ 

Figure \vref{fig:forestation} shows the average forest coverage in the included administrative areas in year 2000. The main points of including it is show that (a) even if only the averages are used for each area, the areas are so small that the figure show quite a lot of detail and (b) an informal visual confirmation that the aggregation algorithm appears to be correct.

<<forestation, fig.cap = "Average forest coverage in the administrative areas in year 2000. Country borders added.", dpi = 400, fig.width=6, fig.height=3>>=
library(sf); library(ggplot2)

ggplot() +
  # graticule behind polygons
  geom_sf(data = grat, linewidth = 0.10) +

  geom_sf(data = my.sf,
          aes(fill = forestation.2000),
          color = NA) +
  geom_sf(data = all.borders, fill = NA, linewidth = 0.05) +

  # lock extent to the bbox
  coord_sf(
    xlim = c(bb["xmin"], bb["xmax"]),
    ylim = c(bb["ymin"], bb["ymax"]),
    expand = FALSE
  ) +

  scale_fill_gradientn(
    colours = rev(terrain.colors(256)),
    limits = c(0, 100),
    name = "Forest coverage (%)  "
  ) +

  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    panel.grid = element_blank(),
    legend.position = "top",
    legend.direction = "horizontal",
    legend.key.width = unit(1, "cm"),
    legend.key.height = unit(0.07, "cm"),          
    legend.text = element_text(size = 7)
  )

@

In year 2000, \num{\Sexpr{round(forestation.2000, 2)}}\% of the total area included in the dataset was covered by forest, in year 2020 that number was down \num{\Sexpr{round(deforestation.2020, 2)}} percentages to \num{\Sexpr{round(forestation.2020, 2)}}\%. The main result is visualised in figure \vref{fig:forestation-change-temporary-5-degrees} which shows the change in average forest coverage for all administrative areas. This zoomed out perspective immediately gives some hints as to were deforestation was most intense, eastern Amazon, Cambodia stand out, but also Nicaragua, Uganda, Tanzania and Mozambique have large administrative area with high levels of deforestation.

<<forestation-change-temporary-5-degrees, fig.cap = paste0("Change in average forest coverage 2000-2020 per administrative area. Country borders added."), fig.width=6, fig.height = 3, dpi = 400>>=
library(ggplot2)

## Prepare the scaling by finding the largest absolute value
m <- max(abs(my.sf$forestation.change.2020), na.rm = TRUE)

ggplot() +
    ## graticule behind polygons
    geom_sf(data = grat, linewidth = 0.10) +

    ## The actual data
    geom_sf(data = my.sf, aes(fill = forestation.change.2020), color = NA) +

    ## lock extent to the bbox
    coord_sf(
        xlim = c(bb["xmin"], bb["xmax"]),
        ylim = c(bb["ymin"], bb["ymax"]),
        expand = FALSE
    ) +

    ## Country borders
    geom_sf(data = all.borders, fill = NA, linewidth = 0.05) +

    ## Coloring
    scale_fill_gradient2(
        low = "#b2182b",
        mid = "white",
        high = "#1a9850",
        midpoint = 0,
        limits = c(-m, m),
        name = "Forest cover change (%)"
    ) +

    theme_minimal() +
    theme(legend.position = "top",
          legend.direction = "horizontal",
          legend.key.width = unit(1, "cm"),
          legend.key.height = unit(0.07, "cm"),          
          legend.text = element_text(size = 7)) + 
    labs(title = NULL)

@

With the data in place, finding administratative areas with high level of deforestation is as simple as ordering the areas by their level of deforestation, see table \vref{tab:highest}, and it is also easy to aggregate to top level regions, see table \vref{tab:highest-top-region}, and to countries, see table \vref{tab:highest-country}.

<<forestation-change-lowest-level-example-table, results = "asis">>=
library(sf); library(terra); library(data.table); library(xtable)
these.regions <- head(my.dt[order(forestation.change.2020), region, ], n = 10)
foo <- my.dt[region %in% these.regions, list(region, f.2000 = round(forestation.2000, 1), fc.2020 = round(forestation.change.2020,1), "Area (\\si{\\kilo\\metre\\squared})" = size.km2), ]
foo <- foo[order(fc.2020), ]
print(xtable(foo,
             digits = c(0, 0, 1, 1, 0),
             caption = "The ten administrative areas with the largest level of deforestation. 'f.2000' = 'the percent of the area that was covered by forest in year 2000', 'fc.2020' = 'forestation change between 2000 and 2020'.",
             label = "tab:highest"),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, sanitize.colnames.function = identity)

@

<<forestation-change-top-level-regions, results = "asis">>=
library(globallivingconditions); library(xtable)
top.level.regions <- my.dt[, list(country = unique(country), f.2000 = unique(round(weighted.mean(forestation.2000, w=size.km2, na.rm = TRUE), 1)), fc.2020 = unique(weighted.mean(forestation.change.2020, w=size.km2, na.rm = TRUE)), size.km2 = sum(size.km2)), by = top.level.region]
foo <- top.level.regions[head(order(fc.2020), n = 10), list(country = iso.3166$String[match(country, iso.3166$String.code)], region = top.level.region, f.2000, fc.2020, "Area (\\si{\\kilo\\metre\\squared})" = size.km2), ]
print(xtable(foo, digits = c(0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0),
             caption = "The ten top level regions with the largest level of deforestation. 'f.2000' = 'the percent of the area that was covered by forest in year 2000', 'fc.2020' = 'forestation change between 2000 and 2020'.",
             label = "tab:highest-top-region"),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, sanitize.colnames.function = identity)
@

<<forestation-change-countries, results = "asis">>=
countries <- top.level.regions[, list(f.2000 = weighted.mean(f.2000, w=size.km2, na.rm = TRUE),
                                      fc.2020 = weighted.mean(fc.2020, w=size.km2, na.rm = TRUE),
                                      "Area ($10^3\\,\\si{\\kilo\\metre\\squared}$)" = sum(size.km2)/1e3
                                      ), by = country]
foo <- countries[head(order(fc.2020), n = 15), ]
foo[, country := iso.3166$String[match(country, iso.3166$String.code)], ]
print(xtable(foo,
             digits = c(0, 0, 1, 1, 0),
             caption = "The 15 countries with the largest level of deforestation. 'f.2000' = 'the percent of the area that was covered by forest in year 2000', 'fc.2020' = 'forestation change between 2000 and 2020'.",
             label = "tab:highest-country"),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, sanitize.colnames.function = identity)
@

\clearpage

\subsection{Consequences of Deforestation}
\label{lab:consequences}
The most basic aspect of the consequences of deforestation is given by the simple question: what replaced the forest? While this question goes beyond the purpose of this paper, we will provide an example of the kind of analyses that are possible once an interesting area, or top level region, is found. For a small area, it is computationally feasible to re-analyse the source data using another algorithm that can give more detailed information about the changes in forest states in this area. Aggregating some of the categories in the source data results in three broad categories: forest, short vegetation, cropland and built-up, and the transitions into and from the state ``forest'' is shown in figure \ref{fig:transitions}.

``What replaced the forest?'' can thus be answered on a cell-by-cell level by a visualisation, see figure \vref{fig:focus-deforestation}, where every cell is tracked and colored based on what happened exactly there, or, on an aggregated level, by calculating the percent of the deforested area that turned up as each of the target categories, see table \vref{tab:example-table} for an example of that kind of statistics.

\tikzset{
  trA/.style={draw=transA, very thick},
  trB/.style={draw=transB, very thick},
  trC/.style={draw=transC, very thick},
  trD/.style={draw=transD, very thick},
  trE/.style={draw=transE, very thick},
  trF/.style={draw=transF, very thick}
}

\begin{figure}
  \centering

\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=10mm and 35mm,
  state/.style={
    draw,
    rounded corners,
    minimum width=3.2cm,
    minimum height=8mm,
    align=center
  },
  lab/.style={
    font=\small,
    fill=white,
    inner sep=1pt
  },
  >={Latex}
]

% nodes
\node[state] (f1) {forest};
\node[state, right=of f1] (b) {short vegetation cover};

\node[state, below=of f1] (f2) {forest};
\node[state, right=of f2] (c) {cropland};

\node[state, below=of f2] (f3) {forest};
\node[state, right=of f3] (s) {built-up};

% arrows with inside labels
\draw[->, bend left=15, trA] (f1) to node[below, sloped, text=transA] {A} (b);
\draw[->, bend left=15, trB] (b) to node[above, sloped, text=transB] {B} (f1);

\draw[->, bend left=15, trC] (f2) to node[below, sloped, text=transC] {C} (c);
\draw[->, bend left=15, trD] (c) to node[above, sloped, text=transD] {D} (f2);

\draw[->, bend left=15, trE] (f3) to node[below, sloped, text=transE] {E} (s);
\draw[->, bend left=15, trF] (s) to node[above, sloped, text=transF] {F} (f3);

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Six transitions to and from the state ``forest''. ``A'' represents deforestation, forest to short vegetation cover, while ``B'' is the inverse: transition from short vegetation cover to forest, ie. forestation. B, D and F represents way that forestation can \emph{increase}, while A, C and E are variants of \emph{deforestation}.}
\label{fig:transitions}
\end{figure}

<<stats.from.polygon>>=
stats.from.polygon <- function(my.region){

    library(sf); library(terra); library(data.table)

    ## For a given region id, get the full state for year 2000 and the full state for year 2020.
    ## find the cells that are instances of any of the six forest transitions
    ## for the other cells, use a simplified ledgend:

    forest_codes <- c(27:48, 127:148)   # >= 5m trees on terra firma + wetlands
    short_vegetation_codes <- c(0:26, 100:126)
    cropland_codes <- c(244)
    forest_codes <- c(27:48, 127:148)
    builtup_codes <- 250
    water_codes  <- c(200:207, 254)     # open surface water + ocean

    ## find the relevant tiles
    ## find the sub polygons in my.sf.split that correspond to this polygon

    my.sub.regions <- which(my.sf.split$region == my.region)
    ## find the tiles that contains at least one element of my.sub.region
    the.tiles <- names(na.omit(sapply(tile_to_poly, function(x) { match(my.sub.regions, x) })))
    ## TODO make this works for multiple tiles.
    the.tile <- the.tiles[1]
    ## For now, just pick the first tile
    my.polygon <- my.sf[which(my.sf$region == my.region), ]
    my.rasters <- sapply(c(2000, 2020), function(year) {
        tile <- file.path(deforestation.path, year, the.tile)
        r <- terra::rast(tile, win = ext(vect(my.polygon)))
        return(r)
    })

    ## Set values outside the polyg to 255 which is not defined
    r1 <- mask(my.rasters[[1]], my.polygon, inverse = FALSE, NA)
    r2 <- mask(my.rasters[[2]], my.polygon, inverse = FALSE, NA)

    r4 <- r2

    ## Stable
    r4[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% forest_codes] <- 7 
    r4[r1 %in% short_vegetation_codes & r2 %in% short_vegetation_codes]  <- 8
    r4[r1 %in% cropland_codes & r2 %in% cropland_codes]  <- 9
    r4[r1 %in% builtup_codes & r2 %in% builtup_codes]  <- 10
    r4[r2 %in% water_codes] <- 11

    ## Change
    r4[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% short_vegetation_codes] <- 1 # A
    r4[r2 %in% forest_codes & r1 %in% short_vegetation_codes] <- 2 # B

    r4[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% cropland_codes] <- 3 # C
    r4[r2 %in% forest_codes & r1 %in% cropland_codes] <- 4 # D

    r4[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% builtup_codes] <- 5 # E
    r4[r2 %in% forest_codes & r1 %in% builtup_codes] <- 6 # F
    r4[r4 > 11] <- 12

    levels(r4) <- data.frame(
        id = 1:12,
        class = c(
            "forest -> short vegetation",
            "short vegetation -> forest",
            "forest -> cropland",
            "cropland -> forest",
            "forest -> built-up",
            "built-up -> forest",
            "stable forest",
            "stable short vegetation",
            "stable cropland",
            "stable built-up",
            "water",
            "other"
        )
    )

    my.tab <- table(values(r4))
    these.rows <- na.omit(match(names(my.tab), levels(r4)[[1]]$id))

    my.df <- levels(r4)[[1]][these.rows, ]
    my.df$frequencies <- my.tab
    my.df$id <- NULL
    ## if "water" is observed, remove it.
    water.is.here <- na.omit(match("water", my.df$class))
    if(length(water.is.here) > 0){
        my.df <- my.df[-water.is.here, ]
    }
    my.df$percents <- round(100 * prop.table(my.df$frequencies), 2)
    my.df <- my.df[order(my.df$percents, decreasing = TRUE), ]
    my.df$frequencies <- NULL

    colnames(my.df) <- c("state or transition", "per cent")
    my.df$type <- c("C", "A", "", "", "", "E", "", "", "B", "D")
    my.df <- my.df[, c(3, 1, 2)]
    return(my.df)
}

@

<<focus-deforestation, fig.cap = "Visualisation of the happened to the deforestated land in the administrative area with the highest level of deforestation.", fig.width = 6, fig.height = 3>>=
forest_codes <- c(27:48, 127:148)   # >= 5m trees on terra firma + wetlands
short_vegetation_codes <- c(0:26, 100:126)
cropland_codes <- c(244)
builtup_codes <- 250

## deforestation.path <- "~/mnt/poverty/media/sf_Our_data/Hans/Onedrive/University\ of\ Gothenburg/Deforestation\ and\ poverty\ -\ Forest\ coverage/5\ degrees/"
my.region <- these.regions[1]
my.sub.regions <- which(my.sf.split$region == my.region)
## find the tiles that contains at least one element of my.sub.region
the.tiles <- names(na.omit(sapply(tile_to_poly, function(x) { match(my.sub.regions, x) })))
## TODO make this works for multiple tiles.
## For now, just pick the first tile
the.tile <- the.tiles[1]
my.polygon <- my.sf[which(my.sf$region == my.region), ]
my.rasters <- sapply(c(2000, 2020), function(year) {
    tile <- file.path(deforestation.path, year, the.tile)
    r <- terra::rast(tile, win = ext(vect(my.polygon)))
    return(r)
})
r1 <- mask(my.rasters[[1]], my.polygon, inverse = FALSE, NA)
r2 <- mask(my.rasters[[2]], my.polygon, inverse = FALSE, NA)

r <- r2

## Order by way of desired apperence in the legend
r[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% short_vegetation_codes] <- 1 # A
r[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% cropland_codes] <- 2 # C
r[r1 %in% forest_codes & r2 %in% builtup_codes] <- 3 # E
r[r2 %in% forest_codes & r1 %in% short_vegetation_codes] <- 4 # B
r[r2 %in% forest_codes & r1 %in% cropland_codes] <- 5 # D
r[r2 %in% forest_codes & r1 %in% builtup_codes] <- 6 # F
r[r > 3] <- 7

levels(r) <- data.frame(
  id = 1:7,
  class = c(
      "A forest \u2192 short vegetation",
      "C forest \u2192 cropland",
      "E forest  \u2192 builtup",
      "B",
      "D",
      "F",
      "non-deforestation"
  )
)

coltab(r) <- data.frame(
  value = 1:7,
  col = c(
      rgb(46,168,153, maxColorValue=255),  # A
      rgb(230,126,34, maxColorValue=255),  # B
      rgb(155,89,182, maxColorValue=255),  # C
      rgb(52,122,183, maxColorValue=255),  # D
      rgb(120,110,100, maxColorValue=255), # E
      rgb(120,140,60, maxColorValue=255),  # F      
      "#F2F2F2"   # 7 other                      (very light grey)
  )
)

plot(r)
plot(my.polygon, add = TRUE, col = NA)

@ 

<<example-table, results = "asis">>=
library(xtable)
my.df <- stats.from.polygon(these.regions[1])
print(xtable(my.df, caption = "Land cover change analysis for the administrative area Kampong Damrei in Chhloung in Krâchéh in Cambodia. This area had the highest level of deforestation and 77 per cent of the total area was deforestated. This table shows what happened to the area that was forest in year 2000. Path 'C', forest to cropland dominated in this area, followed by forest to short vegetation.", label = "tab:example-table"), include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE)
@ 

\section{Discussion}
By aggregating the point data given in \citet{Potapov2022} to administrative areas we have create a data product suitable for analysing both drivers of deforestation and consequences of deforestation. The type of analyses that the data set can be used for ranges from analyses where all areas are of interest, e.g. ``What are the effects of changes in forestation on the living conditions of the people living in the respective areas?'' to studies that focus on areas with high level of deforestation. We have provided two examples of how to analyse such high deforestation areas, visualisations that track changes cell-by-cell and statistical analysis that calculate the distribution of different paths and the proportion non-changing cells in an area of interest.

The division into administrative areas we used was particularly tailed after availability of geo-coded micro data on living conditions provided by the DHS survey programme, and we plan to combine DHS data with the resulting data product in our further analyses of social consequences of deforestation. However, the data product is not tied to DHS data, in fact for many of the included countries, DHS surveys are not available. The areas should still be easy to connect with other data sources, since national statistics generally use administrative areas. In many cases connecting to external data should not require use of any GIS algorithms. This is obviously the case for country level data, where matching should be done using the three letter code given by \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_3166-1_alpha-3}{ISO-3166 alpha-3}, but also for subnational data where the region names published by the GADM project can be used for matching \citep{Hijmans_GADM_v41}.

\section{Acknowledgements}
The project ``Environmental degradation and human wellbeing: On the link between deforestation and living conditions in low- and middle-income countries'' is primarily funded by Marianne and Marcus Wallenberg foundation. University of Gothenburg and University of Birmingham are co-funding the project. 

The data product accompanying this article is based on the data set ``Global Land Cover and Land Use Change, 2000-2020'' \citep{Potapov2022}.

<<export.to.generic.data.format>>=
st_write(my.sf, "forest-change.gpkg", layer = "forest_change", driver = "GPKG", append=FALSE)
file.copy(from = "forest-change.gpkg", to = paste0(path.to.artifacts, "forest-change.gpkg"))
unlink("forest-change.gpkg")
forest.change.dt <- my.dt
colnames(forest.change.dt) <- gsub(".", "_", colnames(forest.change.dt), fixed = TRUE)
write.csv(forest.change.dt, file = "forest-change.csv")
file.copy(from = "forest-change.csv", to = paste0(path.to.artifacts, "forest-change.csv"))
unlink("forest-change.csv")

@ 

